  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 5285559cfa33d7041aa12c3287c4d2a7, type: 3}
  m_Name: 03_UNDERSTANDING_THE_FAULT
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::NotebookSection
  number: 03
  title: UNDERSTANDING THE FAULT
  unlocked: 1
  entries:
  - title: 10. Bit-flip error
    tag: 1
    body: "A <b>bit-flip error</b> happens when a qubit's computational-basis value
      is swapped: |0\u27E9 becomes |1\u27E9, or |1\u27E9 becomes |0\u27E9. In the
      circuit model, it is an unintended X gate. In real hardware, causes include
      stray electromagnetic noise, heat and imperfect control pulses.\n\n<b>Example.</b>
      The system expects a data qubit to stay in |0\u27E9, but an error changes it
      to |1\u27E9. The qubit now differs from the state the computation expects.\n\n<color=#B09A74><b>\u258C
      RESEARCH NOTE</b>\n<indent=0.9em>This exercise considers a <b>single</b> bit-flip
      error. Quantum systems can suffer other kinds of errors too (see entry 20).</indent></color>"
    bench: 1
    qiskit: 'qc.x(data[1])   # the unintended flip (the fault)


      # or as random
      noise in Qiskit Aer:

      from qiskit_aer.noise import pauli_error

      flip
      = pauli_error([("X", 0.05), ("I", 0.95)])'
  - title: 11. Why not just look at the qubits?
    tag: 2
    body: "Measuring a data qubit directly would force it to give a definite 0 or
      1 and would <b>destroy any superposition</b> it carried. If the qubits hold
      protected quantum information, that is exactly what we cannot afford.\n\nThe
      solution is to ask only about <b>relationships</b>: \"do q0 and q1 agree?\"
      rather than \"what is q0?\" The answer to a relationship question does not
      depend on the hidden amplitudes, so the protected information survives.\n\n<color=#E3C58F><b>\u258C
      INTUITION</b>\n<indent=0.9em>Compare a proofreader who checks that two copies
      of a document match <b>without reading what it says</b>. They can spot a mismatch
      without learning, or disturbing, the content.</indent></color>"
    bench: 0
    qiskit: 'qc.measure(anc, syn)    # measure the helpers...

      # qc.measure(data,
      out) # ...never the data mid-run'
  - title: 12. Error correction
    tag: 1
    body: "<b>Error correction</b> means detecting and fixing certain errors while
      preserving the information being protected. It follows four stages:\n\n\u2022
      <b>Encode:</b> spread one piece of information across several qubits.\n\u2022
      <b>Detect:</b> measure carefully chosen checks to get the syndrome.\n\u2022
      <b>Correct:</b> apply the operation that undoes the diagnosed error.\n\u2022
      <b>Verify:</b> confirm that the result is what it should be.\n\n<color=#E3C58F><b>\u258C
      YOUR OBJECTIVE</b>\n<indent=0.9em>Identify the error from the diagnostic results,
      apply the appropriate correction, and verify the outcome.</indent></color>"
    bench: 0
    qiskit: '# encode -> detect -> correct -> verify

      qc.cx(0, 1); qc.cx(0,
      2)          # encode

      qc.measure(anc, syn)              # detect

      qc.x(data[fix])                  
      # correct

      qc.measure(data, out)             # verify'
  guides:
  - label: Build noise models
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/build-noise-models
  - label: Overview of noise management techniques
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/error-mitigation-overview
  - label: Exact and noisy simulation with Qiskit Aer
    url: https://quantum.cloud.ibm.com/docs/en/guides/simulate-with-qiskit-aer
